Sidewatch patch on tree-sitter-markdown v0.5.3 (block grammar), applied to src/grammar.json and regenerated
with tree-sitter CLI 0.25.10 (`tree-sitter generate src/grammar.json`).

An HTML block of type 4 (`<!DOCTYPE html>`, CommonMark: `<!` and a letter, ending at the first line that holds
`>`) never ended when the `>` followed other text on its line: `>` is also a punctuation token of `_line`, whose
right precedence kept extending the line, so the block ran to the end of the document. The closing `>` is now its
own token with lexical precedence 1, so the lexer reads it as the end. In upstream grammar.js (common/html_blocks)
the change is `seq('>', $._close_block)` -> `seq(alias(token(prec(1, '>')), '>'), $._close_block)`.

--- a/src/grammar.json	2026-10-06 22:25:18
+++ b/src/grammar.json	2026-10-06 22:25:18
@@ -2980,7 +2980,19 @@
                 "type": "SEQ",
                 "members": [
                   {
-                    "type": "STRING",
+                    "type": "ALIAS",
+                    "content": {
+                      "type": "TOKEN",
+                      "content": {
+                        "type": "PREC",
+                        "value": 1,
+                        "content": {
+                          "type": "STRING",
+                          "value": ">"
+                        }
+                      }
+                    },
+                    "named": false,
                     "value": ">"
                   },
                   {
